    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/277544962",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **Who this is for:** Everyone on the programme — BAs, Testers, Developers, and Leads.\n\nThis page explains the end-to-end AI-assisted QA methodology used on the Zimmermann SFCC replatform. It covers the four tools, the six-stage process, and where human approval is required before the next stage can begin.\n\nRead this first before using any of the role-specific guides.\n\n---\n\nThe Problem This Solves\n-----------------------\n\n34 integrations × 7 storefronts × 5 core user journeys = **1,190 test scenarios**.\n\nA two-week manual E2E window with a human team covers approximately 200. The remaining 990 go untested before go-live without a structured, automated approach.\n\nThe methodology addresses this by combining human judgement at the points where it is irreplaceable — scenario definition and code review — with AI acceleration at the points where it is not: drafting acceptance criteria, generating test case structures, generating Playwright spec scaffolds from Zephyr cases, classifying defects, and raising fix branches.\n\n---\n\nThe Four Tools\n--------------\n\n| Tool | Role in the Pipeline |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Claude (claude.ai / Claude Desktop)** | AC authoring, Zephyr test case generation, Playwright spec scaffolding via MCP, defect classification, fix investigation |\n| **Zephyr Scale** | Test case management — stores structured test cases linked to Jira stories, tracks execution results |\n| **BrowserStack Automate** | Cross-browser, cross-storefront automated test execution — runs Playwright specs against the 7 storefront × browser matrix |\n| **Jira** | Story and defect tracking — stories link to Zephyr test cases; defects are raised automatically by the triage agent with pre-filled detail |\n\n---\n\nThe Six-Stage Process\n---------------------\n\nDefaultnone┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 1 — Story Intake │\n│ BA prepares the Jira story using the structured story template. │\n│ Includes: user role, goal, acceptance scope, edge cases, regions. │\n└───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘\n│\n┌───────────────────────────────────▼─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 2 — AC Authoring with Claude ◀ HUMAN GATE 1 │\n│ BA retrieves the story via MCP and asks Claude to generate │\n│ Given/When/Then acceptance criteria from it. │\n│ BA reviews every criterion — edits, adds, or rejects before approving. │\n│ Nothing moves to Stage 3 until the BA marks AC as approved in Jira. │\n└───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘\n│\n┌───────────────────────────────────▼─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 3 — Zephyr Test Case Creation via MCP ◀ HUMAN GATE 2 │\n│ Claude retrieves the approved AC via MCP and generates structured │\n│ Zephyr test cases — one per AC item. Tester reviews steps and │\n│ expected results, then marks each test case Ready for Execution. │\n└───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘\n│\n┌───────────────────────────────────▼─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 4 — Playwright Spec Authoring │\n│ Claude retrieves the Zephyr test cases via MCP and generates a │\n│ Playwright spec scaffold from them. Developer adds correct selectors │\n│ to the Page Object file and confirms the spec passes locally. │\n└───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘\n│\n┌───────────────────────────────────▼─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 5 — BrowserStack Execution │\n│ GitHub Actions triggers BrowserStack Automate on PR to release/\\*. │\n│ Matrix: 7 storefronts (AU, UK, US DOM, US INTL, EU DOM, EU INTL, ME) │\n│ × Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge. │\n│ What takes a human team 3 days runs in under 2 hours. │\n└───────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘\n│\n┌───────────────────────────────────▼─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ STAGE 6 — Defect Triage & Fix Branch ◀ HUMAN GATE 3 │\n│ Failed tests trigger the triage agent. Agent classifies each failure: │\n│ code defect / environment issue / test data issue. │\n│ For code defects: agent creates a fix branch, investigates, implements │\n│ a fix (Tier 2–4 files only), and raises a PR. │\n│ No PR is merged without human code review. Tier 1 files: agent stops │\n│ and raises a Jira defect for a developer to investigate manually. │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n---\n\nHuman Gates — What They Mean\n----------------------------\n\nThe pipeline has three mandatory human gates. Nothing advances past a gate until the human action is complete.\n\n| Gate | Where | Action Required |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Gate 1** | End of Stage 2 | BA reviews and approves AC in Claude. Sets Jira story status to **AC Approved** before Zephyr work begins. |\n| **Gate 2** | End of Stage 3 | Tester reviews Zephyr test cases — steps and expected results. Marks test cases as **Ready for Execution** in Zephyr Scale. |\n| **Gate 3** | End of Stage 6 | Developer reviews the agent-raised PR. No merge without code review. Tier 1 file changes are never handled by the agent — a developer owns them. |\n\n---\n\nThe Human-in-the-Lead Principle\n-------------------------------\n\nClaude proposes. Humans decide.\n\n* AC is a draft until a BA approves it\n* Test cases are a draft until a Tester reviews them\n* The Playwright spec scaffold is a starting point until a Developer validates it locally\n* Fix branches are a draft until a Developer merges the PR\n* The agent never touches Tier 1 files (design tokens, shared hooks, runtime config, CI workflows)\n\nThis is not just a philosophy — it is enforced in the pipeline. A Zephyr test case cannot be created from unapproved AC. A PR cannot be merged without review. The Tier 1 file list is enforced by CODEOWNERS in GitHub.\n\n---\n\nRole Summary\n------------\n\n| Role | Stages | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Business Analyst | 1 (story prep), 2 (AC authoring) | Guide 1: Writing Stories & AC with Claude |\n| BA / Tester | 3 (Zephyr test case creation) | Guide 2: Creating Zephyr Test Cases via MCP |\n| Developer / Tester | 4 (spec authoring), 5 (execution), 6 (defect triage) | Guide 3: BrowserStack Execution, Result Sync & Defect Triage |\n\nFor the repo-level Playwright/Gherkin engineering conventions referenced in Stage 4 — directory layout, Page Object Model, multi-region fixtures, secrets required — see [Guide 4: E2E Testing — Playwright & Gherkin Repo Conventions](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305528833).\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f5fa-fe0f",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

